---
import KnowledgeCheck from "../../components/KnowledgeCheck.astro";
import LoopbackCapture from "../../components/LoopbackCapture.astro";
import CourseLayout from "../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const frames = [
  [1, "Client → Server", "SYN", 0, "—", 0, "Client opens the connection."],
  [
    2,
    "Server → Client",
    "SYN, ACK",
    0,
    1,
    0,
    "Server acknowledges the client’s SYN and sends its own.",
  ],
  [
    3,
    "Client → Server",
    "ACK",
    1,
    1,
    0,
    "Client acknowledges the server’s SYN.",
  ],
  [4, "Client → Server", "PSH, ACK", 1, 1, 5, "Client sends HELLO."],
  [
    5,
    "Server → Client",
    "PSH, ACK",
    1,
    6,
    2,
    "Server acknowledges the five bytes and sends OK.",
  ],
  [
    6,
    "Client → Server",
    "FIN, ACK",
    6,
    3,
    0,
    "Client acknowledges OK and finishes sending.",
  ],
  [
    7,
    "Server → Client",
    "ACK",
    3,
    7,
    0,
    "Server acknowledges the client’s FIN.",
  ],
  [8, "Server → Client", "FIN, ACK", 3, 7, 0, "Server finishes sending."],
  [
    9,
    "Client → Server",
    "ACK",
    7,
    4,
    0,
    "Client acknowledges the server’s FIN.",
  ],
];
---

<CourseLayout
  page="wireshark"
  title="Wireshark TCP lab | Computer Networks"
  description="Read a nine-frame TCP capture and identify the handshake, payload, acknowledgement, and close."
>
  <div class="intro lab-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Wireshark lab · TCP</p>
    <h1>
      Read a <span>TCP capture</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Open a small trace in Wireshark and follow five bytes from a client to a
      server. Find the handshake, the data, and the reply.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-links">
      <a
        class="button primary"
        href={`${home}captures/tcp-hello.pcap`}
        download
      >
        Download the sample capture
      </a>
      <a class="button" href="#frame-guide">
        Read the captured frames
      </a>
      <a class="button" href="#live-capture">
        Capture your own TCP traffic
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={home + "labs/wireshark/udp/"}>
        Read the UDP lab
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="filters-heading" class="lab-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="filters-heading">Open and filter the capture</h2>
      <p>
        The capture contains nine frames from a controlled transfer between two
        smoltcp sockets on an in-memory{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#loopback`}>loopback</a> link. The client sends{" "}
        <code>HELLO</code> from port <code>40000</code>. The server on port{" "}
        <code>5001</code> compares those bytes with the expected message and
        replies <code>OK</code>. The public Python file-transfer lab performs a{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#sha-256`}>SHA-256 check</a> before replying.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Open the file.</strong> In Wireshark, choose{" "}
        <strong>File → Open</strong> and select <code>tcp-hello.pcap</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Find the service.</strong> Type <code>tcp.port == 5001</code> in
        the <a href={`${home}#display-filter`}>display filter</a> bar and press
        Enter. Both directions appear because port 5001 can be the source or the
        destination.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Isolate this conversation.</strong> Apply{" "}
        <code>tcp.stream == 0</code> to show just this{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#tcp-stream`}>stream</a>. Add{" "}
        <code>&amp;&amp; tcp.flags.syn == 1</code> to focus on the two
        SYN-bearing frames, or <code>&amp;&amp; tcp.len &gt; 0</code> to focus
        on frames that carry application data. Restore{" "}
        <code>tcp.stream == 0</code> before reading the full exchange.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <details>
      <summary>Find your way around the three panes</summary>
      <ul class="lab-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Packet List.</strong> Each row shows a captured frame. Start
          with frame number, time, direction, and the Info column.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Packet Details.</strong> Expand Internet Protocol to find IP
          addresses and Transmission Control Protocol for ports, flags, and
          sequence numbers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Packet Bytes.</strong> Click a field to highlight its bytes.
          The ASCII column is handy when the payload contains text such as
          HELLO.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="frame-guide" class="lab-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="frame-guide" tabindex="-1">
        Read the exchange
      </h2>
      <p>
        Select a frame and expand <strong>Transmission Control Protocol</strong>
        . Compare the source and destination ports, <strong>Flags</strong>,{" "}
        <strong>Sequence Number</strong>, <strong>Acknowledgment Number</strong>
        , and <strong>TCP Segment Len</strong> with the table. Use Wireshark’s{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#relative-sequence-number`}>
          relative sequence numbers
        </a>{" "}
        for the displayed Seq and ACK values.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="table-hint">Scroll the table sideways to see every column.</p>
    <div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Captured TCP frames"
    >
      <table class="frame-table">
        <caption>TCP capture · relative Seq and ACK numbers</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Frame</th>
            <th scope="col">Direction</th>
            <th scope="col">Flags</th>
            <th scope="col">Seq</th>
            <th scope="col">ACK</th>
            <th scope="col">TCP Len</th>
            <th scope="col">What happened</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {frames.map(
            ([frame, direction, flags, seq, ack, length, meaning]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{frame}</th>
                <td>{direction}</td>
                <td>
                  <code>{flags}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{seq}</td>
                <td>{ack}</td>
                <td>{length}</td>
                <td>{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            ),
          )}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Frames 1–3.</strong> Trace <a href={`${home}#syn`}>SYN</a>, SYN
        + ACK, and <a href={`${home}#ack`}>ACK</a>. The SYN consumes one
        sequence number, so each side starts its payload at relative sequence 1.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Frames 4–5.</strong> Length counts TCP{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#payload`}>payload</a> bytes. The client sends five and
        the server asks for byte 6 next. The very same frame carries two
        application bytes, <code>OK</code>. Right-click frame 4 and choose{" "}
        <strong>Follow → TCP Stream</strong> to see both messages.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Frames 6–9.</strong> Each endpoint sends a{" "}
        <a href={`${home}#fin`}>FIN</a> to finish sending. The other side
        acknowledges it. Use the zero payload lengths to distinguish these
        frames from the <code>HELLO</code> and <code>OK</code> data frames.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <KnowledgeCheck
      id="check-trace-handshake"
      question="Which frame shows the client accepting the server’s starting sequence number?"
      choices={[
        {
          label: "Frame 2",
          feedback:
            "Frame 2 travels from server to client. It acknowledges the client’s SYN and announces the server’s sequence number.",
        },
        {
          label: "Frame 3",
          correct: true,
          feedback:
            "Frame 3 travels from client to server and has ACK 1. That acknowledges the server’s SYN at relative sequence 0.",
        },
        {
          label: "Frame 4",
          feedback:
            "Frame 4 carries HELLO. Look for the client’s first ACK just after the server’s SYN.",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <KnowledgeCheck
      id="check-trace-verdict"
      question="In frame 5, what does ACK 6 tell you?"
      choices={[
        {
          label: "The server’s TCP stack received the client’s five bytes.",
          correct: true,
          feedback:
            "The client started at sequence 1, so the five bytes fill positions 1 through 5. The payload OK carries the application’s response.",
        },
        {
          label: "The server checked a saved file’s SHA-256.",
          feedback:
            "The acknowledgement number tracks received bytes. The lab checks a short message, and the coursework sends its hash verdict in application data.",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <div class="lesson-next">
      <div>
        <h3>Apply what you learned</h3>
        <p>Compare the TCP exchange with individual UDP datagrams.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="button primary" href={home + "labs/wireshark/udp/"}>
        Read the UDP lab
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={`${home}#tcp`}>
        Return to TCP
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LoopbackCapture protocol="tcp" port={5001}>
    <p>
      Prepare the scripts from the{" "}
      <a href={home + "tutorials/tcp-demo/loopback/"}>one-laptop TCP demo</a>.
      With the capture running, start the server and send HELLO. Your trace
      carries a 40-byte file header, five file bytes, and a one-byte application
      receipt. TCP can split or combine these writes, so the frame count and
      sequence numbers can differ from the nine-frame practice file.
    </p>
  </LoopbackCapture>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="tcp-troubleshoot">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="tcp-troubleshoot">What to look for when a transfer fails</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the connection setup, then check data in both directions.
        These patterns help you decide what to check next.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>No matching rows.</strong> Clear the display filter. Check the
        capture interface and port, then start a fresh capture before the client
        connects.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeated SYN with no SYN + ACK.</strong> Verify the address and
        port and check that you can see both directions from your capture point.
        Check the server listener and any firewall on the practice network.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>RST during setup.</strong> Try <code>tcp.flags.reset == 1</code>{" "}
        and check who sent the reset. A SYN followed by a reset is consistent
        with a refused connection. Check the server’s listening port.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Retransmission or duplicate ACK.</strong> Try{" "}
        <code>
          tcp.analysis.retransmission || tcp.analysis.fast_retransmission
        </code>{" "}
        or <code>tcp.analysis.duplicate_ack</code>. These are Wireshark
        annotations based on the frames it saw. Compare sequence ranges and
        timings before drawing conclusions about loss.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>TCP works, but the client gets no OK.</strong> Follow the stream
        and check the server terminal. ACK numbers track the byte stream. The
        file verdict comes from the program after it checks and saves the file.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChAdvTCPAnalysis.html">
        How Wireshark analyzes TCP
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sources-heading" class="lab-section">
    <h2 id="sources-heading">References</h2>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.wireshark.org/docs/dfref/t/tcp.html">
          Wireshark TCP display filter fields
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChAdvFollowStreamSection.html">
          Wireshark guide to following a stream
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html#section-3.4">
          RFC 9293 · TCP sequence numbers
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/smoltcp-rs/smoltcp/tree/v0.12.0">
          smoltcp 0.12.0 · TCP stack and loopback packet writer
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CourseLayout>
